{
  "id": "COMPOUND:42136 - PROTEIN:5854",
  "verbalization": "Neoclovene is shown to have a target relationship with LNCaP through multiple pathways, all of which point to MIP as a common target. The evidence suggests that Neoclovene interacts with MIP, which is then implicated in the regulation of LNCaP. This relationship is supported by the presence of multiple MIP targets, including compounds like (1S,3R,6S,8R,11S,12S,15R,16R)-15-[(2R)-4-[(2R)-3,3-dimethyloxiran-2-yl]butan-2-yl]-7,7,12,16-tetramethylpentacyclo[9.7.0.01,3.03,8.012,16]octadecan-6-ol and 2-[4-[3-(3,4-Dimethoxyphenyl)-1,3,3a,4,6,6a-hexahydrofuro[3,4-c]furan-6-yl]-3-methoxyphenoxy]-6-(hydroxymethyl)oxane-3,4,5-triol, all of which are known to target LNCaP. The consistent presence of MIP as a common target across these pathways strengthens the hypothesis that Neoclovene exerts its effects through MIP, which in turn influences LNCaP. The mechanism is further supported by the fact that MIP is a known target of multiple compounds, and its involvement in LNCaP suggests a potential role in cellular processes that are dysregulated in this cancer type. The evidence is robust and consistent, providing a clear, mechanistic link between Neoclovene and LNCaP.",
